1
00:00:00,150 --> 00:00:03,137
Three commands. Only one belongs now.

2
00:00:03,307 --> 00:00:08,128
Pip, our simulated campus delivery rover, receives a fresh
command,

3
00:00:08,238 --> 00:00:11,715
an old command, and a command it has already handled.

4
00:00:11,885 --> 00:00:16,173
They look similar on screen. Treating them alike would be a
mistake.

5
00:00:16,343 --> 00:00:21,335
Have you ever wondered how a connected agent preserves the
meaning of a message?

6
00:00:21,505 --> 00:00:27,863
Chapter five, Applications, connects local control, shared
services, and message context.

7
00:00:29,889 --> 00:00:37,164
You will separate a fast local control loop from shared
analysis, coordination, and training services.

8
00:00:37,334 --> 00:00:45,590
You will explain why messages need context such as identity,
time, sequence, units, and policy version.

9
00:00:45,760 --> 00:00:50,474
You will also connect delayed learning feedback to the action
that produced it.

10
00:00:50,644 --> 00:00:56,383
Welcome to the tutorial on networking for behaviorist AI by Blue
Impulse.

11
00:00:56,553 --> 00:00:59,369
Connect the subtopics Real-time response,

12
00:00:59,479 --> 00:01:04,364
Networking for a behaviour-based agent, and Where should the
intelligence run?

13
00:01:06,933 --> 00:01:09,386
Start with Chapter two, Behaviorism

14
00:01:09,496 --> 00:01:16,131
and Behaviorist AI. A reactive agent may map a sensed condition
directly to an action.

15
00:01:16,301 --> 00:01:24,514
A fixed rule such as avoiding a detected obstacle can produce
useful behaviour without learning from reward.

16
00:01:24,684 --> 00:01:31,361
Reinforcement learning adds a different claim: experience is
used to improve a policy.

17
00:01:31,531 --> 00:01:36,481
A network connection does not turn a fixed rule into a learning
algorithm,

18
00:01:36,591 --> 00:01:39,727
and not every connected agent learns online.

19
00:01:39,897 --> 00:01:42,798
For our campus delivery system example,

20
00:01:42,908 --> 00:01:48,775
think of a locally evaluated policy with separate, reviewed
updates from a server.

21
00:01:48,945 --> 00:01:56,283
We will label architectural choices clearly instead of treating
them as properties of every AI system.

22
00:01:59,628 --> 00:02:06,135
Time-critical sensing, policy evaluation, and actuator control
can stay near the device.

23
00:02:06,305 --> 00:02:10,892
Our robot observes an obstacle, evaluates its current policy,

24
00:02:11,002 --> 00:02:14,479
and sends a control signal to the actuator hardware.

25
00:02:14,649 --> 00:02:18,596
This local path need not wait for every cloud exchange.

26
00:02:18,766 --> 00:02:24,739
A real-time task must meet a specified deadline, not just be
fast on average.

27
00:02:24,909 --> 00:02:31,053
Local processing removes some network dependency, but it still
needs adequate compute,

28
00:02:31,163 --> 00:02:34,683
trustworthy sensing, and suitable physical controls.

29
00:02:34,853 --> 00:02:38,800
Local does not automatically mean safe. The requirements

30
00:02:38,910 --> 00:02:42,622
and failure behaviour still have to be designed and checked.

31
00:02:46,063 --> 00:02:53,231
In our illustrative campus delivery system, each robot handles
immediate motion control locally.

32
00:02:53,401 --> 00:03:01,870
A shared service can allocate tasks, coordinate inventory, and
analyse selected telemetry from several devices.

33
00:03:02,040 --> 00:03:08,782
Imagine a local driver following a route while a dispatcher
coordinates deliveries across the fleet.

34
00:03:08,952 --> 00:03:11,213
The driver needs immediate awareness;

35
00:03:11,323 --> 00:03:17,104
the dispatcher benefits from a broader view. The analogy
separates responsibilities.

36
00:03:17,274 --> 00:03:20,794
The network may distribute reviewed policy updates,

37
00:03:20,904 --> 00:03:25,363
but a received update should not automatically bypass
validation.

38
00:03:25,533 --> 00:03:33,256
Edge and cloud are not opposing teams. Put each part of the
workload where its deadlines and resource needs fit.

39
00:03:36,672 --> 00:03:44,416
A message needs enough context to be interpreted correctly. A
number by itself is not a complete instruction.

40
00:03:44,586 --> 00:03:52,479
Device identity tells us which source or destination it
concerns. A timestamp helps determine freshness.

41
00:03:52,649 --> 00:04:00,756
A sequence number can expose repeated or out-of-order messages.
Units prevent confusing metres with centimetres.

42
00:04:00,926 --> 00:04:06,323
A policy version connects an action or update to the model that
produced it.

43
00:04:06,493 --> 00:04:08,797
These fields are useful evidence,

44
00:04:08,907 --> 00:04:16,075
but fields alone do not authenticate a sender. Security checks
must support the claimed identity.

45
00:04:16,245 --> 00:04:23,925
Before acting, the receiver needs a policy for rejecting stale,
unexpected, or unauthorised commands.

46
00:04:24,095 --> 00:04:29,386
Pip is a story name; robot-seven is the device identity used in
this

47
00:04:29,496 --> 00:04:34,850
demonstration. A receiver must check the identity expected by
the protocol,

48
00:04:34,960 --> 00:04:36,923
not guess from a friendly label.

49
00:04:41,367 --> 00:04:47,127
Run the supporting packet-checking example. This is an
illustrative receiver design,

50
00:04:47,237 --> 00:04:49,904
not a universal network protocol.

51
00:04:50,074 --> 00:04:56,474
Our toy receiver expects robot seven, metres as the unit, and
policy version three.

52
00:04:56,644 --> 00:05:04,303
For this demonstration, it rejects commands older than two
hundred milliseconds and sequences already processed.

53
00:05:04,473 --> 00:05:08,675
The first message has the expected context, a new sequence,

54
00:05:08,785 --> 00:05:12,924
and an age of fifty milliseconds. It passes these checks.

55
00:05:13,094 --> 00:05:18,427
The second is six hundred milliseconds old. The receiver rejects
it as stale,

56
00:05:18,537 --> 00:05:21,481
even if the network eventually delivered it.

57
00:05:21,651 --> 00:05:25,619
The third repeats an already processed sequence. The receiver

58
00:05:25,729 --> 00:05:30,551
rejects it as a duplicate instead of executing the command
twice.

59
00:05:30,721 --> 00:05:35,542
Our example assumes authentication was checked elsewhere. It
does not

60
00:05:35,652 --> 00:05:41,497
implement cryptographic authentication or certify a
safety-critical controller.

61
00:05:41,667 --> 00:05:45,763
The useful lesson is why delivery alone is insufficient:

62
00:05:45,873 --> 00:05:50,076
the receiver must decide whether the message is still
meaningful.

63
00:05:54,933 --> 00:05:59,455
Delayed feedback must be associated with the action that
produced it.

64
00:05:59,625 --> 00:06:02,057
Suppose action forty-two turns left,

65
00:06:02,167 --> 00:06:08,375
and action forty-three moves forward. Their feedback packets
arrive in the opposite order.

66
00:06:08,545 --> 00:06:14,647
Updating whichever action happened most recently would connect
evidence to the wrong decision.

67
00:06:14,817 --> 00:06:21,003
Use identifiers and recorded context to match each result with
its original state

68
00:06:21,113 --> 00:06:23,567
and action before a learning update.

69
00:06:23,737 --> 00:06:31,225
If a feedback packet is lost, the evidence is missing. That does
not silently mean the reward was zero.

70
00:06:31,395 --> 00:06:36,877
This is how networking details can affect the data used by a
learning algorithm,

71
00:06:36,987 --> 00:06:40,145
even when policy evaluation stays local.

72
00:06:43,454 --> 00:06:48,532
Now inspect the outage condition for our illustrative delivery
system.

73
00:06:48,702 --> 00:06:56,232
The design must say whether the robot pauses, completes a
limited task, or enters another defined state.

74
00:06:56,402 --> 00:07:03,570
Continuing safely during a link outage cannot simply be assumed
because some computation is local.

75
00:07:03,740 --> 00:07:07,559
Consider expiring permissions, missing coordination,

76
00:07:07,669 --> 00:07:12,298
conflicting routes, and how the system reconnects after the
interruption.

77
00:07:12,468 --> 00:07:19,252
Record what happened locally so the shared service can reconcile
state when communication returns.

78
00:07:19,422 --> 00:07:25,801
The right fallback depends on the task and its hazards. Our
tutorial demonstrates the reasoning

79
00:07:25,911 --> 00:07:28,791
rather than certifying a physical robot.

80
00:07:31,976 --> 00:07:38,611
Pause and think. The robot performed action forty-two, but its
reward packet never arrived.

81
00:07:38,781 --> 00:07:45,906
Should the learner record a reward of zero? And should a
repeated command be executed again automatically?

82
00:07:52,076 --> 00:07:56,065
No to both. Missing evidence is not an observed zero,

83
00:07:56,175 --> 00:08:00,591
and a duplicate needs the receiver's defined handling policy.

84
00:08:00,761 --> 00:08:08,889
Associate any later feedback with the original action. Decide
how long to wait and how to record missing data.

85
00:08:09,059 --> 00:08:15,203
Then explain why a timestamp alone does not prove a message came
from an authorised sender.

86
00:08:15,373 --> 00:08:23,032
Use the packet demonstration to change an age or sequence and
predict which validation rule should reject it.

87
00:08:26,759 --> 00:08:34,225
Keep suitable time-critical work local, and use the network for
telemetry, coordination, and reviewed updates.

88
00:08:34,395 --> 00:08:37,041
Messages need interpretable context

89
00:08:37,151 --> 00:08:44,681
and appropriate authentication. Stale or repeated commands must
not be treated as automatically valid.

90
00:08:44,851 --> 00:08:53,257
Delayed learning feedback belongs to its original action. Lost
evidence must not silently become zero reward.

91
00:08:53,427 --> 00:08:59,912
Decide which responsibility belongs on the robot, at a nearby
gateway, or in the cloud.

92
00:09:00,082 --> 00:09:04,861
Finally, distinguish a fixed reactive rule, a deployed learned
policy,

93
00:09:04,971 --> 00:09:08,491
and an algorithm currently updating that policy.

94
00:09:08,661 --> 00:09:11,583
Draw the local and shared responsibilities,

95
00:09:11,693 --> 00:09:16,600
then explain your outage behaviour before calling the
architecture complete.

96
00:09:16,770 --> 00:09:21,847
A message can be perfectly formatted and still be too late,
duplicated,

97
00:09:21,957 --> 00:09:27,461
or unauthorised. Meaning depends on the receiver's state and the
surrounding checks.

98
00:09:31,976 --> 00:09:35,731
Pip's small problem now has an explanation you can test.

99
00:09:35,901 --> 00:09:41,789
Use Chapter 5, Applications, and the named subtopics to revisit
the reasoning.

100
00:09:41,959 --> 00:09:45,372
The tutorial guide includes the full teaching steps

101
00:09:45,482 --> 00:09:51,242
and the checked practice answers. Run the supporting
demonstration and change one input.

102
00:09:51,412 --> 00:09:56,745
Blue Impulse. Follow the signal, predict the result, and check
your explanation.
